gzD(){return"Does LastStats scrobble my music?"},
gzp(){return"No. LastStats is a visualisation app: it displays the scrobbles already recorded on your Last.fm account, but it does not record any itself.\n\nTo automatically scrobble your music, use a dedicated app such as Pano Scrobbler (available on Android)."},
gzJ(){return"Does the app work on macOS or other platforms?"},
gzv(){return"LastStats is developed and tested on Android. Behaviour on other platforms (macOS, Windows, Linux\u2026) is unverified, bugs or unexpected behaviour may occur."},
gzK(){return"Is LastStats open source?"},
gzw(){return"Yes! The source code is freely available on GitHub. The project is independent, built with passion by SanoBld. Feel free to contribute, report bugs, or leave a star \u2b50."},
gzL(){return"Where is my data stored?"},
gzx(){return"On your device only. LastStats has no server: your scrobbles are cached locally for fast access, and your Last.fm credentials are stored locally too. Nothing is sent anywhere except Last.fm's official API."},
gzM(){return"How do I enable favorites?"},
gzy(){return"Go to Settings > Account and enter your Last.fm secret key (you can find it next to your API key at last.fm/api/accounts), then follow the steps on screen. Once connected, you can favorite tracks directly from the app. This is not available with the app's built-in key."},
gzN(){return"What is a 'scrobble'?"},
gzz(){return"A scrobble is a track logged as played on your Last.fm account \u2014 it's Last.fm's own term for 'one counted listen'. All your totals (top artists, stats, etc.) are based on it."},
gzO(){return"How do levels and achievements work?"},
gzA(){return"Your account level grows with your total scrobble count (there's no max level). Cards also get a border (bronze \u2192 iridescent) based on how many times that artist/track/album has been played. Everything is computed automatically from stats already cached locally, with no extra network calls."},
gzP(){return"How does power saving mode work?"},
gzB(){return"Power saving mode reduces what the app does to save battery: tilt parallax is turned off, the screen refresh rate is capped at about 60 Hz, background updates run less often, and animated artwork and badge shine are paused. Images, exports and share cards keep full quality.\n\nYou can keep it always on, make it follow your phone's own battery saver, or have it turn on below a battery level you choose. You'll find it in Settings > Power saving mode."},
gzE(){return"How do I back up or restore my data?"},
gzq(){return"Go to Settings > Backup. You can export a backup file, with or without your Last.fm key as you prefer, and import it later on this phone or on another device to get your settings back."},
gzF(){return"Does the app work offline?"},
gzr(){return"Yes, to some extent. Stats already loaded stay available offline thanks to local caching, but a connection is still needed to fetch new scrobbles."},
gzG(){return"Can I switch Last.fm accounts?"},
gzs(){return'Yes, you can save up to 3 Last.fm accounts. From Settings > Account, tap "Add an account" and then switch between them whenever you like. The local cache is automatically reset when you switch, so the data of two accounts never gets mixed.'},
gzH(){return"How do I set up notifications?"},
gzt(){return"From Settings > Notifications, you can turn on an alert when a sync finishes, choose how often alerts appear, or turn notifications off completely if you prefer."},
gzI(){return"Artwork is missing or keeps loading. What can I do?"},
gzu(){return"Clear the cache in the app (Settings > Cache), then in Android (Settings > Apps > LastStats > Storage > Clear cache). If images are still missing, back up your data (Settings > Backup), uninstall and reinstall the app, then restore your backup."},
